WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.375
Hi, I'm Neymar Odaner, and this is Module 2 of OS

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.140
Work.

3
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.095
In Module 1, you learned to see AI as a system

4
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.280
of parts, and to delegate with limits.

5
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.955
Now, we'll look at the places where that work happens.

6
00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.440
The chat, work, and desktop.

7
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.935
You learned to recognize when a short question is enough, turn

8
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.860
a vague request into a work order with a clear finish,

9
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.400
confirm what the AI actually read, know where the task runs,

10
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.975
write a delivery contract, and check what you got back.

11
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.755
There's no terminal in this module, and the practices work in

12
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.315
the chat you already use or in a notepad, even without

13
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.840
work or desktop on your account.

14
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.235
Denise and Lucia are still with us.

15
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.875
When I ask, pause the video and do the practice right

16
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:44.515
then.

17
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.360
Not every task calls for a big tool.

18
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.115
Setting up a complicated routine for something that only needed an

19
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.140
answer wastes time and gives you more to check.

20
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.180
The chat is a conversation interface.

21
00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.935
Think of the question you ask a colleague in the hallway

22
00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:58.640
between classes.

23
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.615
It's short, the answer comes right away, and you decide what

24
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.635
to do with it.

25
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.315
If the answer doesn't work, you don't start over, you send

26
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.380
an adjustment in the same conversation.

27
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.155
Lucia was covering a colleague's math class and asked for three

28
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.580
ways to explain fractions using the ingredients of a cake recipe.

29
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.135
She got half a cup of sugar, a quarter stick of

30
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.860
butter, and a third of a cup of milk.

31
00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.615
She asked to swap the third one for three eggs split

32
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.540
between two recipes, and got it.

33
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.080
One and a half eggs each.

34
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:28.575
That's the chat.

35
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.780
Question, answer and adjustment.

36
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.000
A loose question gets an encyclopedia answer.

37
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.255
When you say who will read it and what they'll do

38
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.275
with the text, the answer comes out at the right length

39
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.400
and in the right tone.

40
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:45.375
Denise needed to explain the class council to the parents of

41
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:45.455
6th graders.

42
00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:50.475
She just asked what a class council is and got a

43
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.660
technical paragraph, correct, but far too long for a note.

44
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.360
Then she asked for the explanation in two sentences.

45
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.010
for sixth grade parents, because it was going in a note

46
00:02:01.035 --> 00:02:01.630
home.

47
00:02:01.655 --> 00:02:03.515
She got two simple sentences.

48
00:02:03.635 --> 00:02:05.890
The class council is a meeting of the teachers with the

49
00:02:05.915 --> 00:02:09.390
coordinators to talk about how each class and each student is

50
00:02:09.415 --> 00:02:13.295
doing, and it produces agreements to help whoever needs it.

51
00:02:13.675 --> 00:02:17.870
Same subject, now at the length and in the tone of

52
00:02:17.895 --> 00:02:18.255
the note.

53
00:02:19.015 --> 00:02:23.530
Every short question leaves gaps, and the AI fills those gaps

54
00:02:23.555 --> 00:02:24.915
without telling you.

55
00:02:25.615 --> 00:02:29.255
It assumes the age of the class, the tone, the length.

56
00:02:29.575 --> 00:02:33.090
That's why you ask it to list what it assumed.

57
00:02:33.115 --> 00:02:36.615
Lucia asked for an opening question for her lesson on mixtures,

58
00:02:37.275 --> 00:02:40.670
and the list of assumptions showed the AI had pictured high

59
00:02:40.695 --> 00:02:41.255
school students.

60
00:02:42.255 --> 00:02:45.870
She corrected it in one line, the class is 8th grade,

61
00:02:45.895 --> 00:02:47.570
and the question was adjusted.

62
00:02:47.595 --> 00:02:50.150
So, when is the chat enough?

63
00:02:50.175 --> 00:02:53.410
If what you need is an answer you read and check

64
00:02:53.435 --> 00:02:55.890
on the spot, the chat handles it.

65
00:02:55.915 --> 00:02:59.370
If it's a document with several sources, a conversation falls short.

66
00:02:59.395 --> 00:03:02.430
Pause the video now and use this template with a real

67
00:03:02.455 --> 00:03:05.470
question from this week, with no student names.

68
00:03:05.495 --> 00:03:08.670
Read the list of assumptions first and correct one of them

69
00:03:08.695 --> 00:03:09.910
in a short message.

70
00:03:09.935 --> 00:03:13.190
And when the task doesn't fit in a conversation, a job

71
00:03:13.215 --> 00:03:16.635
with several sources needs a definition of done.

72
00:03:16.775 --> 00:03:19.430
Without one, the AI may keep researching when you only needed

73
00:03:19.455 --> 00:03:22.190
to compare three options, and you get back a long text

74
00:03:22.215 --> 00:03:23.570
you don't know where to start reviewing.

75
00:03:23.595 --> 00:03:26.330
In work, you don't talk sentence by sentence.

76
00:03:26.355 --> 00:03:29.570
You hand over a task, like an analysis or a document,

77
00:03:29.595 --> 00:03:30.990
and get the result back to review.

78
00:03:31.015 --> 00:03:33.870
Think of ordering a cake from a bakery.

79
00:03:33.895 --> 00:03:36.590
You say the flavor, the size, and the day.

80
00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:39.250
And you don't stand there watching the oven.

81
00:03:39.275 --> 00:03:40.810
An important note.

82
00:03:40.835 --> 00:03:43.635
Work isn't available on every plan.

83
00:03:43.775 --> 00:03:46.270
If you can't find it on yours, place the work order

84
00:03:46.295 --> 00:03:47.370
in the regular chat.

85
00:03:47.395 --> 00:03:49.090
And it works the same way.

86
00:03:49.115 --> 00:03:52.055
What changes is the request, not the tool.

87
00:03:52.255 --> 00:03:55.530
Denise had to hire the bus for the museum field trip.

88
00:03:55.555 --> 00:03:58.850
And she had three quotes to compare and bring to the

89
00:03:58.875 --> 00:03:59.415
principle.

90
00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.960
A work order says five things.

91
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.235
The goal, which is what the result is for, the inputs,

92
00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:09.855
the material the AI can use, the output, the format of

93
00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.415
what comes back, the limits, what it shouldn't do, and the

94
00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.595
stop, when the work is over.

95
00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:19.340
It looks like the authorization from lesson 6, and it's related.

96
00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.775
The authorization says what the AI can do, and the work

97
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.015
order says what must exist at the end.

98
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.615
Denise wrote the one for the bus in five lines, in

99
00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:28.620
three minutes.

100
00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.195
She got back a three-row table with only what was in

101
00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.095
the quotes.

102
00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:37.795
And where a quote said nothing, it showed not informed instead

103
00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:38.895
of a guess.

104
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:42.515
That means the document doesn't cover it, not that the risk

105
00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.440
is zero.

106
00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:45.880
The fifth part deserves attention.

107
00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.775
Without a stop, the AI decides on its own when it's

108
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:49.380
done.

109
00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.180
Sometimes it stops early, but often it goes way too far.

110
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:56.875
Lucia asked for a comparison of microscope kits for the lab,

111
00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:00.320
Just like that, with no limit, and got 5 pages.

112
00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.955
With kits from stores she'd never heard of.

113
00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.295
Then she limited it to 3 kits, to the catalogs she

114
00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.560
pasted, and to one table.

115
00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:10.980
With deliver and stop at the end.

116
00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:14.295
She got 1 page, 3 rows, ready to check, all from

117
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.735
the material she provided.

118
00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:16.835
Pause the video now.

119
00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.115
Pick a research task you'd do this week, gather 3 fictional

120
00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.100
or public options, and fill in the 5 parts.

121
00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.135
Send it in work, if your account has it, or in

122
00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.355
the regular chat.

123
00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:30.455
Check that only your options came back in the format you

124
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.600
asked for, with not informed wherever data was missing.

125
00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:36.000
The work order mentions files.

126
00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.080
But can the AI really read each one?

127
00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.960
Many task failures are access failures.

128
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:42.900
You imagine?

129
00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.120
The AI can see a folder that was never shared.

130
00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:49.960
And the answer talks about documents the model didn't read.

131
00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.675
Desktop is the app that lives on your computer.

132
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.635
If your app offers it, it can open folders and other

133
00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:57.860
apps, always with your permission.

134
00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.850
But having the app doesn't give it access to all your

135
00:06:00.875 --> 00:06:01.835
documents.

136
00:06:02.035 --> 00:06:05.375
In the browser chat, you attach or paste each file.

137
00:06:05.495 --> 00:06:07.635
There are different paths with the same rule.

138
00:06:07.795 --> 00:06:09.655
The AI only reads what reached it.

139
00:06:10.935 --> 00:06:14.210
Lúcia uses the browser chat on the school computer and desktop

140
00:06:14.235 --> 00:06:15.495
on her laptop at home.

141
00:06:16.115 --> 00:06:19.290
And in both, she checks what the AI received before asking

142
00:06:19.315 --> 00:06:20.190
for the work.

143
00:06:20.215 --> 00:06:23.670
Giving access is like handing over the key to one drawer,

144
00:06:23.695 --> 00:06:24.995
not to the whole school.

145
00:06:25.235 --> 00:06:27.495
Put only what the task needs in a subfolder.

146
00:06:28.075 --> 00:06:31.475
And when the app asks for access, choose just that subfolder.

147
00:06:32.235 --> 00:06:34.330
Screen names change between versions.

148
00:06:34.355 --> 00:06:37.575
What doesn't change is choosing the smallest folder possible.

149
00:06:39.075 --> 00:06:42.390
Denise's Science Fair folder had three versions of the budget, and

150
00:06:42.415 --> 00:06:45.715
she wanted to test the AI without risking the original.

151
00:06:46.215 --> 00:06:49.850
She allowed only a subfolder of samples with two copies.

152
00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:52.990
Before the comparison, she asked for the list of files the

153
00:06:53.015 --> 00:06:55.890
AI could read, with the first and last line of each

154
00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:56.595
one.

155
00:06:56.855 --> 00:06:58.975
A name alone doesn't prove it was read.

156
00:06:59.015 --> 00:07:01.470
The first and last line show that the beginning and the

157
00:07:01.495 --> 00:07:04.195
end came through, and you check them against the file.

158
00:07:04.675 --> 00:07:07.830
If the list is wrong, the problem is access, not the

159
00:07:07.855 --> 00:07:08.235
request.

160
00:07:08.935 --> 00:07:12.190
And what if desktop isn't on your account, or can't reach

161
00:07:12.215 --> 00:07:13.135
the folder?

162
00:07:13.235 --> 00:07:14.575
Deliver the files away that works.

163
00:07:15.495 --> 00:07:17.830
Attach them in the chat, with the button next to the

164
00:07:17.855 --> 00:07:21.070
message box, or paste the text with a header that has

165
00:07:21.095 --> 00:07:21.710
the file name on top.

166
00:07:21.735 --> 00:07:24.990
That's what Lúcia did with the procedure for a water and

167
00:07:25.015 --> 00:07:27.310
oil experiment and the materials list.

168
00:07:27.335 --> 00:07:30.630
She asked for the list before the summary, and the AI

169
00:07:30.655 --> 00:07:33.870
gave back both names with the right first and last lines.

170
00:07:33.895 --> 00:07:37.255
Pause the video and set up your practice folder.

171
00:07:37.855 --> 00:07:44.310
In Documents, create the folder Trano with two text files, a

172
00:07:44.335 --> 00:07:48.175
visual A and a visual B, with three made-up lines each.

173
00:07:48.235 --> 00:07:51.710
Attach or paste them in the chat and ask for the

174
00:07:51.735 --> 00:07:53.755
list, without summarizing it.

175
00:07:53.835 --> 00:07:54.715
Check names and lines.

176
00:07:55.275 --> 00:07:57.850
If something's wrong, send them again and ask for the list

177
00:07:57.875 --> 00:07:58.135
again.

178
00:07:58.700 --> 00:07:59.940
The file was read.

179
00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:02.640
But where does the work happen?

180
00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.920
And what happens if you close your laptop?

181
00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:08.135
A task doesn't keep going just because it started on a

182
00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:09.195
modern screen.

183
00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:11.135
If you don't know where it runs, you don't know why

184
00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.640
it stopped, or where to look for the result.

185
00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.820
In local execution, the work runs on your machine.

186
00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.040
It's like a cake in your oven at home.

187
00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.480
If the power goes out, the oven stops.

188
00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.720
Power, network, and your computer's permissions all matter.

189
00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:29.695
Lucia asked desktop to review the lesson plans that only exist

190
00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:30.900
on her laptop.

191
00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:34.355
The task ran on the laptop, read the folder with the

192
00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.415
term's lesson plans, and saved to that same folder.

193
00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.815
At 6 in the evening, she closed the lid and went

194
00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:40.600
home.

195
00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.880
The next day, the review had stopped halfway through.

196
00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.540
In the cloud, the work runs on remote computers.

197
00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.400
It's like the bakery.

198
00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:52.075
The oven doesn't depend on your house, but the baker only

199
00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:53.740
has the ingredients you brought.

200
00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:55.415
the chat you already use is an example.

201
00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.068
The model works on the company's computers and that's why it

202
00:08:59.093 --> 00:09:01.788
only knows what you pasted or attached.

203
00:09:01.813 --> 00:09:04.028
Denise wants the draft of the attendance report to be ready

204
00:09:04.053 --> 00:09:06.548
even if she turns off her laptop.

205
00:09:06.573 --> 00:09:08.728
For that, the spreadsheet needs to be somewhere the cloud can

206
00:09:08.753 --> 00:09:09.828
reach.

207
00:09:09.853 --> 00:09:12.128
Neither one is better.

208
00:09:12.153 --> 00:09:15.708
Local depends on your computer being on, with network, and it

209
00:09:15.733 --> 00:09:18.228
reads the folders on your machine that you allow.

210
00:09:18.253 --> 00:09:21.268
The cloud depends on the tool and your plan, and it

211
00:09:21.293 --> 00:09:24.848
It only reads the file sent or connected to it.

212
00:09:24.873 --> 00:09:27.408
Each one has a dependency you need to know.

213
00:09:27.433 --> 00:09:29.128
Watch out for a common assumption.

214
00:09:29.153 --> 00:09:32.448
I started it in work, so it runs on its own.

215
00:09:32.473 --> 00:09:35.448
Some environments keep going without your machine on.

216
00:09:35.473 --> 00:09:38.668
But that depends on the feature, not on the name of

217
00:09:38.693 --> 00:09:39.068
the screen.

218
00:09:39.093 --> 00:09:42.148
When in doubt, look it up on the tool's help page.

219
00:09:42.173 --> 00:09:43.428
Or test it.

220
00:09:43.453 --> 00:09:46.828
Start a short task, close your laptop for 10 minutes and

221
00:09:46.853 --> 00:09:48.428
see if it moved forward.

222
00:09:48.453 --> 00:09:53.328
Denise wrote down on the project sheet created in lesson 5

223
00:09:53.353 --> 00:09:56.828
where the report runs, where it reads from and where it

224
00:09:56.853 --> 00:09:57.673
saves.

225
00:09:57.933 --> 00:09:59.602
And she wrote a pending item.

226
00:09:59.627 --> 00:10:02.762
She doesn't know how long the draft stays stored in the

227
00:10:02.787 --> 00:10:03.482
task.

228
00:10:03.507 --> 00:10:07.822
With that, she went to ask the school support team, pause

229
00:10:07.847 --> 00:10:10.542
the video and do the same for one of your tasks,

230
00:10:10.567 --> 00:10:13.462
on the sheet or in a notepad.

231
00:10:13.487 --> 00:10:17.282
Whatever you don't know becomes a pending item, along with where

232
00:10:17.307 --> 00:10:18.642
you look for the answer.

233
00:10:18.667 --> 00:10:22.442
Now, let's bring the request, the files and the check together

234
00:10:22.467 --> 00:10:24.322
in a single text.

235
00:10:24.347 --> 00:10:26.342
The delivery contract.

236
00:10:26.367 --> 00:10:28.382
Think of the work order at a repair shop.

237
00:10:28.407 --> 00:10:29.867
It has fixed fields.

238
00:10:29.907 --> 00:10:33.522
The device, the problem, what not to touch and how to

239
00:10:33.547 --> 00:10:34.962
test it at pickup.

240
00:10:34.987 --> 00:10:36.507
Nobody writes fix it well.

241
00:10:37.787 --> 00:10:41.262
The delivery contract is the work order from lesson 8 with

242
00:10:41.287 --> 00:10:42.462
a 6-part verification.

243
00:10:42.487 --> 00:10:49.622
So, you have go, inputs, output, limits, verification and stop.

244
00:10:49.647 --> 00:10:51.002
One sentence each.

245
00:10:51.027 --> 00:10:54.147
The authorization from module one fits inside it.

246
00:10:54.287 --> 00:10:58.282
Goal and stop stay, files become inputs, actions and time become

247
00:10:58.307 --> 00:10:58.847
limits.

248
00:10:59.347 --> 00:11:02.295
Denise filled in the six fields in four minutes, for the

249
00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:05.515
draft minutes of the teaching staff meeting based on the agenda.

250
00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:09.435
They're fields of a work order, not magic words.

251
00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:13.235
Write the minutes leaves, open the format, the length, and what

252
00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:14.555
to do about what's missing.

253
00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:18.115
Look at what happened when Denise asked for good minutes, with

254
00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:19.800
only the agenda pasted in.

255
00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.495
She got minutes of a meeting held on March 12th at

256
00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:26.055
2 in the afternoon in the teacher's lounge.

257
00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:29.155
Date, time and place weren't in the agenda.

258
00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:31.520
The AI filled them in with a guess.

259
00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.655
With the six fields, the answer changed.

260
00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:37.415
She got one line per item, with Lucia on the science

261
00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.415
fair calendar and Marcos on math tutoring.

262
00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:43.755
The date was marked as not informed and so was the

263
00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:47.215
third item, which had no one responsible in the agenda.

264
00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:49.335
At the end, the verification.

265
00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:52.235
The three items and The two people responsible are in the

266
00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:54.835
minutes and nothing was sent.

267
00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:59.395
The contract settled the format, the length and what's missing and

268
00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:00.820
the AI stopped guessing.

269
00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:04.740
Good, clear and complete are wishes.

270
00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:06.480
And nobody can check a wish.

271
00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.135
Verification uses criteria that anyone can check by looking at the

272
00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:13.080
result, like the quality ruler from lesson 4.

273
00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.660
Lucia used to ask for well-explained experiment procedures.

274
00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:17.720
She swapped that for two criteria.

275
00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:22.055
Each step starts with a verb, and every material mentioned in

276
00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.820
the steps is on the materials list.

277
00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.700
Now she checks each procedure in a minute.

278
00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:29.875
And the easier it is to check, the sooner a slip

279
00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:30.675
shows up.

280
00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:34.740
In Denise's minutes, the verification said two people responsible.

281
00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.435
She counted in the agenda and in the minutes two and

282
00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:42.135
two in 30 seconds because the minutes were a list.

283
00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:45.035
Pause the video and fill in a contract with a made-up

284
00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:46.220
five-item agenda.

285
00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:47.920
No real names.

286
00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:52.055
Send it in the chat, check one criterion directly against the

287
00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:55.480
agenda, and save the answer for the next lesson.

288
00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:56.680
The AI said it's done.

289
00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:58.135
Is it really done?

290
00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:01.655
the word done doesn't show that the file opens or that

291
00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:03.140
the data was kept.

292
00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:07.596
A small check done by you usually finds more problems than

293
00:13:07.621 --> 00:13:09.676
asking again to make it better.

294
00:13:09.701 --> 00:13:12.676
It's like checking your grocery bag against the receipt before you

295
00:13:12.701 --> 00:13:13.356
leave.

296
00:13:13.381 --> 00:13:15.496
It's not enough for the bag to be full.

297
00:13:15.521 --> 00:13:18.176
Every item on the receipt has to be there and nothing

298
00:13:18.201 --> 00:13:19.101
extra.

299
00:13:19.561 --> 00:13:22.556
Lucia got the minutes from the science department meeting and compared

300
00:13:22.581 --> 00:13:24.521
each decision with the agenda.

301
00:13:24.601 --> 00:13:28.156
For the lab schedule, the person responsible was herself, same as

302
00:13:28.181 --> 00:13:29.256
the agenda.

303
00:13:29.281 --> 00:13:32.956
For buying reagents, the agenda said not defined, and the minutes

304
00:13:32.981 --> 00:13:36.476
had someone named Paulo, a made-up name, found in two minutes

305
00:13:36.501 --> 00:13:37.396
of comparing.

306
00:13:37.421 --> 00:13:39.296
The minutes looked complete.

307
00:13:39.321 --> 00:13:42.436
Only the line-by-line comparison showed the error.

308
00:13:42.461 --> 00:13:44.256
You don't need to reread everything.

309
00:13:44.281 --> 00:13:45.256
Pick three points.

310
00:13:45.281 --> 00:13:49.456
A fact that matches the source, a format that's what you

311
00:13:49.481 --> 00:13:53.376
asked for, and an action that works or was done the

312
00:13:53.401 --> 00:13:54.516
way you agreed.

313
00:13:54.541 --> 00:13:57.796
In the attendance report, Denise checked whether the total absences

314
00:13:57.821 --> 00:14:01.316
for 7th grade A matched the sum in the spreadsheet, whether

315
00:14:01.341 --> 00:14:04.316
the file opened with one column per month and whether the

316
00:14:04.341 --> 00:14:06.796
spreadsheet link, mentioned in the report, opened.

317
00:14:06.821 --> 00:14:09.636
It's also worth asking the AI for evidence.

318
00:14:09.661 --> 00:14:11.776
I reviewed everything and it's correct.

319
00:14:11.801 --> 00:14:13.738
Doesn't say what was reviewed.

320
00:14:13.763 --> 00:14:16.058
Ask it to show what it checked side by side with

321
00:14:16.083 --> 00:14:18.578
the source and to say what it didn't check.

322
00:14:18.603 --> 00:14:22.318
A test that was run isn't a test that was suggested.

323
00:14:22.343 --> 00:14:25.038
And even the AIs I checked, it is a claim.

324
00:14:25.063 --> 00:14:27.598
Spot check one item on the list.

325
00:14:27.623 --> 00:14:31.078
Finally, write down what you observed in each check with the

326
00:14:31.103 --> 00:14:32.198
exact result.

327
00:14:32.223 --> 00:14:33.258
Found an error?

328
00:14:33.283 --> 00:14:37.878
Fix the request to before you reuse it or the source

329
00:14:37.903 --> 00:14:40.478
if the error came from there.

330
00:14:40.503 --> 00:14:43.538
Otherwise, the error comes back next time.

331
00:14:43.563 --> 00:14:48.058
Lucia corrected the name in the minutes and added a line

332
00:14:48.083 --> 00:14:49.958
to the delivery contract.

333
00:14:49.983 --> 00:14:54.498
Anyone responsible that the agenda doesn't define becomes not informed.

334
00:14:54.523 --> 00:14:58.278
And if you don't find any error at all, that's a

335
00:14:58.303 --> 00:14:58.778
great sign.

336
00:14:58.803 --> 00:15:02.278
Write down what you compared it with, because the log shows

337
00:15:02.303 --> 00:15:04.918
you looked, not just that you trusted it.

338
00:15:04.943 --> 00:15:06.238
Pause the video now.

339
00:15:06.263 --> 00:15:10.738
Take the delivery you saved in lesson 11, or the practice

340
00:15:10.763 --> 00:15:12.743
agenda and minutes from this lesson.

341
00:15:13.123 --> 00:15:16.299
Check a fact, a format and an action, like something sent

342
00:15:16.324 --> 00:15:20.679
without permission, and write three notes with the result of each.

343
00:15:20.704 --> 00:15:22.044
Let's put it all together.

344
00:15:23.584 --> 00:15:26.719
For a small question, the chat is enough, as long as

345
00:15:26.744 --> 00:15:30.099
you say who it's for and what it's for, and ask

346
00:15:30.124 --> 00:15:31.299
for the assumptions.

347
00:15:31.324 --> 00:15:35.139
For a bigger task, you write a work order with goal,

348
00:15:35.164 --> 00:15:38.239
inputs, output, limits and stop.

349
00:15:38.264 --> 00:15:42.139
Before the work, you confirm what the AI read, with the

350
00:15:42.164 --> 00:15:45.079
name and the first and last line.

351
00:15:45.104 --> 00:15:48.379
You know where the task runs, reads and saves.

352
00:15:48.404 --> 00:15:51.259
And whatever you don't know becomes a pending item.

353
00:15:51.284 --> 00:15:54.919
You bring it all together in a delivery contract with a

354
00:15:54.944 --> 00:15:56.959
verification someone else can check.

355
00:15:56.984 --> 00:16:00.459
And you only call it done after checking a fact, a

356
00:16:00.484 --> 00:16:02.064
format and an action.

357
00:16:02.164 --> 00:16:05.544
When you have about 30 minutes, do the module's optional lab,

358
00:16:06.484 --> 00:16:09.279
from loose question to work order in the additional material for

359
00:16:09.304 --> 00:16:09.844
lesson 12.

360
00:16:10.624 --> 00:16:13.584
In module 3, terminal and codecs in practice.

361
00:16:14.204 --> 00:16:18.659
The delivery contract comes along, now with an AI that works

362
00:16:18.684 --> 00:16:19.744
in a folder of yours.

363
00:16:20.764 --> 00:16:21.422
See you there.
